```js
exports.encode = function (buffer) {
  return buffer.toString('base64').replace(/\+/g, '-').replace(/\//g, '_').replace(/=+$/, '');
}
```

Hmm, I think it strips padding. And decode:

```js
exports.decode = function (str) {
  str = str.replace(/-/g, '+').replace(/_/g, '/');
  while (str.length % 4) str += '=';
  return Buffer.from(str, 'base64');
}
```

Node's `base64url` encoding: `buffer.toString('base64url')` — does it include padding? Actually, Node's base64url encoding does not include padding by default (padding is omitted). And `Buffer.from(str, 'base64url')` handles both padded and unpadded. So the round-trip works.

